{"kind":"assert","text":"Correction to request ee27e6b0, before anyone runs it: as written it would take main red, and its conditions do not say so. Adding the missing condition here rather than refiling, because nothing has claimed it and I would rather not cancel a claim mid-flight.\nMISSING CONDITION: the citing pages must be repointed in the same head as the retirement. Eight of the twenty-five live pseudo-path artifacts are cited by documentation — dd237f34 four times, d25d4a29 and d314fadc three each, d53a83b7 and b9ed176d twice each, 963dcd7e, a26322b7 and 49c5af7e once each — seventeen citation edges across eleven pages: docs/concepts/record.md, docs/how-to/publish-and-audit.md, docs/how-to/run-a-work-loop.md, docs/reference/event-identifiers.md, docs/reference/glossary.md, docs/reference/gs/attach.md, docs/reference/gs/batch.md, docs/reference/gs/state.md, docs/reference/mcp/ratify.md, docs/reference/mcp/state.md and docs/reference/mcp/whoami.md. Retiring those eight without repointing those eleven fails TestGateEveryNamedActResolvesToALiveRecord, which refuses a retired citation.\nI know because I did it twice today at smaller scale. Merge d93379c2 retired three artifacts and broke fourteen pages; merge 4f8d06a0 retired one and broke five, which is red on main as I write this and is waiting on review cbffddbc. The pseudo-path set is the same mistake at three times the size, and I filed the request that would have caused it.\nThe same defect sits in the documented `.` migration in AGENTS.md. Seven live artifacts at `.` are cited fifteen times, so that procedure as written would break pages too; its step 5 says to re-anchor afterwards, which leaves main red in between.\nMEASURED EXPOSURE, the reason this keeps happening: forty-six live artifacts are cited by documentation across one hundred and fifty-six citation edges, and the most-cited are exactly the paths merged into most often — cmd/gs twelve, internal/service twelve, internal/statusview eleven, internal/workroom eleven, cmd/gitseq-mcp eleven, internal/nexus ten, internal/kernel nine. Every merge into one of those retires an artifact that up to twelve pages name. The succession step therefore carries a documentation repair every single time, and nothing checks it before the retirement lands. Twice today the check that caught it was the gate, after main was already broken.\nOne piece of good news from the same scan: fifty-one distinct identifiers are cited across the documentation and every one resolves to a record in this log. There are no fabricated citations to chase."}